timestamp=2026-11-04T16:14:55.544Z level=INFO run=4cbc268f message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Also check other dispatches on 2023-07-18 after the breach? Later dispatches at 09:44 etc - Meat and Dry\n# The breaches affected Frozen (02:00-04:00) and Meat (00:00 onward spikes). \n# Goods already dispatched earlier might be affected if stored, but dispatch is when they leave warehouse? \n# \"Expected Revenue (PLN) — total value of goods dispatched from the warehouse that reached customers as sales. Goods that were\n# disposed of due to spoilage must not be counted as revenue.\"\n# Spoilage happens in storage; if temperature control failed, goods in storage could spoil. \n# But what gets \"disposed of due to spoilage\" with a trace? If the entire batch dispatched at 03:17 is affected by the breach, it's likely written off. That matches as a major disposal event.\n# Are there other violations? Only that window. That’s the only unmistakable spoilage event. Maybe nothing else?\n# Total dispatched value (reconstructed correctly with EAN mapping): earlier we got 55391.0. The 03:17 batch is 10000. \n# If we exclude that, expected revenue would be 55391 - 10000 = 45391. Is that all?\n# Let us be sure. Also check for other patterns - maybe other cold chain breaks? \n# Also look at temperatures more broadly. For example, any other readings where temp > max_safe? We saw only 6 violation readings total, all on 18th. That’s a clear single incident.\n# Also \"Every unit dispatched from the warehouse during July 2023 was either sold or written off due to spoilage. No other outcomes (theft, returns, inter-store transfers) occurred.\" \n# So only the spoiled ones (with unmistakable trace) are excluded.\n# That fits: one major spoilage incident on 18th affecting the batch dispatched at 03:17. That’s it?\n# Let us also look at the exact violations again - Frozen violations were at 02:00 and 04:00 (-3.13, -2.64), very high (bad for frozen). Meat violations at 00:00, 02:00, 04:00, 06:00 - extreme (7.23 up to 16.26). \n# So the cold chain was broken for several hours that night. Any goods dispatched before/during/after? The batch at 03:17 is right in the middle - definitely exposed. Later dispatches same day (09:44 Meat, 16:17 Meat, 19:48 Dry) - if they were dispatched after the breach was discovered and goods potentially compromised? Or were they from different stock that survived? But the breach is over by 06:00? Temperatures go back to normal after that? Let us check.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
