timestamp=2026-11-04T16:15:21.031Z level=INFO run=4cbc268f message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# After 06:00, temps recover to normal. So later dispatches (09:44 Meat - ESMJ8RTM, 16:17 Meat - JQQZB92E) are from stock stored under normal conditions? Or were they affected?\n# It depends on whether the spoiled goods are only those that were in the affected storage zone during the breach and then dispatched, or if the entire affected inventory is written off. \n# But the task says \"Goods that were disposed of due to spoilage must not be counted as revenue. Disposal write-offs are rare and, when they occur, leave an unmistakable trace in the data. Only exclude a dispatch from revenue if you are certain it represents a disposal event — when in doubt, count it as a sale.\"\n# A specific dispatch batch being written off is a clear disposal event. Also maybe multiple dispatches? But only that one big batch at 03:17 is right in the breach window. The others are hours later after recovery - presumably from different/new inventory or unaffected lots. That seems plausible.\n# Also the batch at 03:17 contains a lot of frozen and meat items (total 10000). That’s consistent with a major loss from that breach.\n# Another possibility: all dispatches of meat/frozen products on 2023-07-18 are suspect? Or just that batch? Let us look at their exact times and what else was dispatched.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
