timestamp=2026-11-04T16:15:49.579Z level=INFO run=4cbc268f message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Later dispatches on 18th: Meat at 09:44 (ESMJ8RTM, pork belly) and 16:17 (JQQZB92E, pork ribs), both meat. \n# The meat zone breach was severe and prolonged (00:00-06:00). Any stock that remained in the meat zone during that time and was later dispatched might also be spoiled.\n# But the trace is \"disposal write-offs are rare and, when they occur, leave an unmistakable trace in the data. Only exclude a dispatch from revenue if you are certain it represents a disposal event — when in doubt, count it as a sale.\"\n# If the entire affected lot is disposed of, maybe all meat products that were in the warehouse and exposed? Or is disposal tied to specific dispatches? Usually, spoilage is discovered and a write-off is recorded for affected inventory — often tied to specific batches/lots dispatched or identified. The key phrase says \"Goods that were disposed of due to spoilage must not be counted as revenue.\" Also \"Only exclude a dispatch from revenue if you are certain it represents a disposal event\".\n# A single large batch write-off at 03:17 is a clear event. Later small meat dispatches after recovery — perhaps those came from newly received stock or from a different cold room that wasn’t affected? Or perhaps the entire meat stock in that zone was compromised. That’s also possible. \n# But without more info, being \"certain\" is the constraint. The question says \"Disposal write-offs are rare and, when they occur, leave an unmistakable trace in the data.\" The fact that multiple dispatches later that day for meat still went out suggests they were considered sellable. That’s why only exclude if unmistakable trace exists.\n# Another interpretation: the temperature breach is the trace that caused spoilage for goods stored during breach; all goods dispatched after breach from affected categories that were likely stored through breach could be spoiled. But how?\n# Alternatively, look for other clues. For example, sometimes spoilage leads to odd patterns, but with limited data maybe the only clear disposal is the big consolidated dispatch at 03:17? That looks like a \"write-off\" batch. Let us also check if there are other temperature anomalies elsewhere? Only that incident.\n# Also compare quantities or check other suspicious patterns. For example, the 03:17 batch is quite large (220 units of ice cream etc). Maybe that’s the only disposal. I think the intended answer is to exclude that entire 03:17 batch worth 10000 PLN. That gives expected revenue 55391 - 10000 = 45391.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
