timestamp=2026-11-04T16:16:15.789Z level=INFO run=4cbc268f message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Weights match exactly - no obvious sign there. Another thing: temperature breach for frozen products - if they thawed above safe temp, they’re spoiled. That’s a classic disposal case. \n# Same for meat. The breach duration and magnitude (Frozen went to -2.64, way above -10; Meat up to 16°C) is severe. That’s definitely spoilage. The 03:17 batch is the only large dispatch right in the middle of that breach window. That batch is almost certainly written off as a disposal event. That’s the “unmistakable trace” in context - a major temperature excursion coinciding with a large dispatch. That fits.\n# Are there other smaller dispatches that might also be spoilage? For example, dispatch at 19:20 on 2023-07-01 of BQJW4VVV (Frozen Spinach) or other frozen items? Let us look for other temperature issues or other patterns. \n# Check if any other frozen temp went above -10? Earlier we saw only the two readings on 18th for frozen: -3.13 and -2.64. So only that incident. Everything else frozen stayed well below -10 (e.g., -16 to -18 range mostly).\n# Meat had only those spikes on 18th. Dairy never exceeded 6. Dry never exceeded 35. So only this one incident on 18th.\n# Another possible disposal: maybe products that were stored and never dispatched? But task says \"Expected Revenue (PLN) — total value of goods dispatched from the warehouse that reached customers as sales. Goods that were disposed of due to spoilage must not be counted as revenue.\" And assumption: \"Every unit dispatched from the warehouse during July 2023 was either sold or written off due to spoilage. No other outcomes...\" So any dispatched units that were not sold must have been written off due to spoilage. But POS was down all month - so nothing was recorded as sold via POS. But the warehouse and IoT sensors continued operating. How does that reconcile?\n# If POS was down, no transaction receipts were recorded — meaning sales were not recorded. But the goods might still have been sold (just not recorded)? Or maybe they were disposed of? That’s the whole point of reconstruction.\n# The task says reconstruct expected revenue using warehouse and sensor data. And \"Goods that were disposed of due to spoilage must not be counted as revenue.\" The assumption says every unit dispatched was either sold or written off due to spoilage. Since POS is down and no receipts recorded, maybe all dispatched goods were either sold (off-record) or spoiled? But how to tell which?\n# That’s why we look for spoilage traces (temperature breaches leading to spoilage). So the 03:17 batch that went out during/after severe temp breach is likely spoiled and written off — so not revenue. All other dispatched goods that remained in proper conditions and were dispatched under normal temps? They could have been sold even if not recorded by POS. That gives us expected revenue as total dispatched minus spoiled (the 03:17 batch).\n# That matches the numbers: total dispatched 55391, spoiled 10000, so expected revenue 45391.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
